<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Liked Your Presentation Because... – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Books and Movies</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Liked Your Presentation Because...</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Interpret characters' motives in short stories. Reading: Identify the main ideas of persuasive texts and their arguments. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by practicing minimal pair sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can interpret characters' motives in short story descriptions by answering questions about key decisions and actions (e.g., "Why did the character move to another city?" "Because his/her family got a new job"). Can identify the main ideas of persuasive texts about books or movies (e.g., "What is the main message of this movie review?"). Can accurately identify and produce sounds in minimal pairs (e.g., distinguishes between /ʊ/ in "full" and /uː/ in "fool").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 40%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a few short sentences or phrases on the board or projector, each describing a very brief plot of a book or movie. Students read them silently and quickly identify the genre (e.g., "A detective solves a mystery in an old house." -> "Mystery"). This activates prior knowledge of genres and vocabulary like *plot* and *character* from Lesson 1.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models reading a short paragraph describing a character's decision, focusing on intonation and clarity. The teacher then models identifying and distinguishing minimal pair sounds /ʊ/ and /uː/ by clearly pronouncing pairs like "full" and "fool", pointing to written words simultaneously. The teacher also models the pronunciation of third-person singular verbs ending in "-s," "-es," and "-ies" within the context of a sentence (e.g., "He *watches* movies and *eats* popcorn.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key vocabulary and concepts through questions.
                    <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is a 'plot' in a story?" (Students might respond: "It's what happens, the events.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a movie is 'engaging,' does it keep your attention or make you bored?" (Students: "It keeps your attention.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Books and Movies".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher provides students with a short, authentic-looking movie review (approximately 100-120 words) from a student blog or school newspaper, focusing on the scenario "Books and Movies". The review should contain key vocabulary (e.g., *movie*, *character*, *plot*, *theme*, *entertaining*, *engaging*, *emotional*, *genre*, *liked*, *watched*, *enjoyed*) and grammar structures (present simple for preferences, past simple for plot summary, "verb + -ing + noun + because").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Read and interpret the main ideas and arguments in a short persuasive text (movie review).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the movie review silently.
                    <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is the main topic of this text?" "Is the reviewer recommending the movie or not?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read statements about the review and mark them true or false (e.g., "The reviewer thinks the plot was boring.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match descriptions of events or characters from the review to their corresponding effects or motivations (e.g., "Character X decided to leave the city" -> "because he wanted a new life.").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Interpret main ideas and character motives in short stories/reviews. Identify persuasive arguments. Practice differentiating minimal pair sounds in reading. Practice present simple for preferences and reasons ("I like reading because..."), past simple for plot descriptions, and comparing preferences ("I prefer... over... because...").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Gap-fill:</b> Students receive a worksheet with short paragraphs (e.g., character descriptions or mini-reviews) with blanks. They fill in the blanks using a word bank containing target vocabulary (e.g., *character*, *plot*, *theme*, *engaging*, *prefers*, *because*) and verbs in the correct tense (present or past simple). E.g., "The main _______ was brave because she _______ to save her village."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice - Motive Match:</b> Students read 3-4 short scenarios describing a character's action. For each scenario, they choose the most appropriate motive from a given list or complete a sentence to express the motive using "because." E.g., "Character A decided to move to another city. Why did he move?" (Students complete: "Because his family got a new job.") This directly targets interpreting characters' motives.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction - Minimal Pair Identification & Main Idea Scan:</b> In pairs, students receive a list of sentences, each containing one of two minimal pair words (e.g., "The *full* basket was heavy." / "The *fool* made everyone laugh."). The teacher reads a sentence aloud, and students must quickly identify and circle the correct word in their sentence. Then, they scan a new short persuasive text (e.g., a short book blurb) and underline the main argument for reading it and one key reason, discussing their findings using "I think the main idea is..." and "I prefer action movies over comedies because they are more thrilling."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Books and Movies".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> "Reading Response: My Movie/Book Choice." Students are given three different short descriptions or reviews of either books or movies (e.g., a fantasy book synopsis, a documentary review, and a short story plot summary). Each text is about 80-100 words.
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read all three texts individually.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For each text, they identify: 1) the main idea/message (e.g., "What is the main message of this book description?"), 2) the likely genre, and 3) a potential motive for a character's key action mentioned (if applicable, e.g., "Why did the character decide to explore the unknown planet?").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After analyzing all three, students choose ONE book or movie description they find most *engaging* or *entertaining*.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They then write a short paragraph (3-4 sentences) explaining their choice. They must use phrases like "I prefer _______ because it is _______," "I like reading/watching _______ because _______," and refer to elements like the *plot*, *characters*, or *theme* from the description. They should also compare it briefly to another option (e.g., "I prefer this fantasy book over the documentary because it is more thrilling.").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in reading and comprehension, accurate interpretation of main ideas and character motives, and application of target grammar and vocabulary to express informed preferences based on reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during pair work, and collection of student "Reading Response: My Movie/Book Choice" paragraphs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their written paragraph from Stage 4, along with their answers to the comprehension questions for each of the three texts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher):</b>
                    <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Reading Comprehension:</b> Accurately identifies main ideas and arguments of persuasive texts (e.g., movie/book reviews).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Character Interpretation:</b> Correctly interprets characters' motives from short story descriptions.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary Use:</b> Effectively uses target vocabulary (e.g., *plot*, *theme*, *character*, *engaging*, *entertaining*, *prefer*) in responses.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Accuracy:</b> Demonstrates correct use of present simple for preferences and reasons, past simple for story descriptions, and comparing preferences.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness (Identification):</b> Accurately selects correct words from minimal pairs based on context.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two short reflective prompts on an exit ticket:
                    <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most interesting main idea you identified in a text today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new reason you used today to explain why you like or prefer a book/movie?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one strategy you used today to understand a character's motives?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can identifying the main idea of a review help you decide what to watch or read?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students successfully identified the main ideas of the persuasive texts and interpreted characters' motives. The minimal pair identification activity was effective, but some students still struggled with distinguishing the sounds when reading. I need to provide more explicit modeling and perhaps a quick listening-based reading check in the next lesson's warm-up. The grammar points (present simple for preferences, past simple for plot) were generally well-applied. The activity allowed for the use of "I like this book because it's exciting" and "I prefer action movies over comedies because they are more thrilling."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The understanding gained from reading and analyzing book/movie reviews and expressing preferences will be directly applied in Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction). Students will use these ideas to discuss their favorite books/movies and present their opinions orally to peers.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>